---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/paths";
---

<Layout title="About" description="What Peculiar Interests is, where the data comes from, and how it is read.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Federal MPs and senators must declare their registrable interests within 28 days of being sworn in, and notify
      any change within 28 days of it happening: property, shares, trusts, directorships, debts, accounts, other
      assets and income, gifts, sponsored travel, and memberships that could give rise to a conflict. Spouses and
      dependent children are included. The registers exist so the public can see where a parliamentarian's private
      interests might meet their public duties.
    </p>
    <p>
      The registers are public, but not easy. The House publishes one PDF per member; the Senate a web app. Neither
      offers a download, a search across people, or a way to see what changed last month. Peculiar Interests reads
      both into one schema, publishes the result as browsable pages and downloadable data, and keeps the history in
      git so every change is dated and attributable.
    </p>

    <h2>How the data is read</h2>
    <p>
      The Senate register is read field by field from the data service behind the official web app. Most House
      statements are system-generated PDFs whose tables are extracted exactly from the text layer, with no model
      involved. A handful of House statements are handwritten scans; those are transcribed by a locally run
      text-recognition model and then checked by a person before they are shown as confirmed. Every person page
      says which of these applies to it and links to the official document.
    </p>
    <p>
      The text is the parliamentarian's own. We normalise placeholders like "Not Applicable" to an empty item and
      group identical names for the <a href={withBase("/mentions")}>mentions</a> pages, and otherwise leave the
      wording alone.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      If an entry here does not match the official document, that is our error and we want to fix it: open an issue
      on <a href="https://github.com/benswift/pollie-watch/issues">GitHub</a> or email
      <a href="mailto:ben.swift@anu.edu.au">ben.swift@anu.edu.au</a> with the person's name and what is wrong. If
      the official document itself is wrong or out of date, the place to raise that is the
      <a href="https://www.aph.gov.au/Senators_and_Members/Members/Register">Registrar of Members' Interests</a> or
      the
      <a href="https://www.aph.gov.au/Parliamentary_Business/Committees/Senate/Senators_Interests"
        >Senate Standing Committee of Senators' Interests</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Who</h2>
    <p>
      A <a href="https://cybernetics.anu.edu.au">Cybernetic Studio</a> project by
      <a href="https://benswift.me">Ben Swift</a> at the ANU School of Cybernetics, and a sibling of the
      <a href="https://apsaitracker.app">Australian Government AI Tracker</a>. Code is MIT, the dataset is CC BY 4.0.
      Not affiliated with the Parliament of Australia or any party.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
